{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.CanonicalOrderingSaddle","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.CanonicalOrderingSaddle","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrderingSymmetry","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.List.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Perm.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Count","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.canonical_not_extremal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.canonical_not_extremal","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L176-L198","doc":"**No extremal principle selects the canonical arrangement.**\n\nRestated as the claim it is used for: within the *same isotope* — the same\nmultiset of nucleon species, so the same `(Z, N)` — there is an arrangement\nstrictly below the canonical one and an arrangement strictly above it. Hence the\ncanonical arrangement is neither the minimiser nor the maximiser of the\nproduction interface product, and a variational selection rule of either sign\ncannot pick it out.\n\nScope-(b) exclusion, and it excludes exactly one candidate: *extremality*. It\nsays nothing about selection rules of other kinds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.canonical_saddle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.canonical_saddle","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L141-L174","doc":"**The canonical arrangement is strictly interior.**\n\nFix two protons and two neutrons. If the mixed factor is smaller than the p-p\none and larger than the n-n one — `f_nn < f_pn < f_pp`, which is what the\nsubstrate anchor delivers — then\n\n* `pnnp` (which trades the canonical `pp` interface for a second `pn`) sits\n  strictly **below** the canonical `ppnn`, and\n* `nppn` (which trades the canonical `nn` interface for a second `pn`) sits\n  strictly **above** it.\n\nThe two trades push in opposite directions precisely because `f_pn` lies\n*between* `f_pp` and `f_nn`. The canonical arrangement is therefore a saddle of\nthe arrangement-indexed product, not an extremum of it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd_deuteron_ordering_independent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd_deuteron_ordering_independent","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L126-L133","doc":"**Null control.** The deuteron's two arrangements have the *same* single\ninterface, so the product is invariant — exactly, for every `f`. The ordering\nfreedom does not exist at `A = 2`; it switches on at `A ≥ 3` with both species\npresent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaces_ppnn_not_perm_ifaces_pnnp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaces_ppnn_not_perm_ifaces_pnnp","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L118-L124","doc":"Hence the two interface lists are not even permutations of one another: the\n`ifaceProd` values are genuinely allowed to differ, and §3 shows they do. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaces_not_determined_by_species_counts","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaces_not_determined_by_species_counts","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L102-L116","doc":"Two arrangements of **two protons and two neutrons** — the same isotope — with\ndifferent interface multisets: `ppnn ↦ [pp, pn, nn]` carries a `pp` interface,\n`pnnp ↦ [pn, nn, pn]` carries none.\n\nSo \"the observable depends only on the interface multiset\" does **not** make the\nobservable a function of `(Z, N)`. The freedom is merely re-coordinatised: from\n*which arrangement* to *which multiset*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd_eq_of_ifaces_perm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd_eq_of_ifaces_perm","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L89-L98","doc":"**The product is a function of the interface multiset alone.** Rearranging\nthe interface list — by any permutation whatever — leaves the product fixed.\n\nThis is the exact premise of the \"the physical datum is the multiset, so the\nordering is a redundant labelling\" resolution. It is true. §2 shows why it is\nnot enough. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.NucleonOrdering.ifaceProd","kind":"def","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/CanonicalOrderingSaddle.lean#L83-L87","doc":"The per-interface product of `closed_form_tau_M.closed_form_ratio`, with the\narrangement supplied instead of hardcoded: one factor `f` per adjacent pair,\nindexed by that pair's interface type. ","signatureChunk":0}]}